{
 "summary": "I resumed SP2 on Android in the review-sync worktree. The WIP plus the merged mainline compiled cleanly and its 316 sync tests passed before I changed anything. Most of SP2 was already there: all 20 policy units, the S6-02 seam (fake socket, virtual clock, fake reachability, separate validation and persistence dispatchers), the close/status/nack tables, backoff, watchdogs, pacer, receive budget, pause handling, hello-epoch recovery, chat fence and history caps, and v2 casing and tie-break. I checked every SP2 rule in plans/04 against the code. Each defect below got a failing test first, then a fix.\n\n**Defects fixed in main code:**\n- **Pacer window leak and deadlock** (`SyncOutboundPacer`): editing an object while its previous copy was still unacknowledged left the old copy's bytes in the 1 MiB window. The old copy's ack can never match again, so the bytes never came back. Small edits slowly shrank the window. Two copies of a ~500 KB object deadlocked all mutations until the next reconnect. A newer copy now takes over the old copy's slot.\n- **Duplicate tombstone after a pause**: a delete still unconfirmed when the screen locked was sent twice on return, once at the old stamp under a `wire:` key and once at a newly reserved stamp. The rebuilt wire-ID index now includes `forcedLocalDiff` IDs, so it goes out once.\n- **Identity collision** (contract 2.1, previously iOS only): a remote waypoint reusing a local drawing's UUID, or the reverse, produced two objects under one ID and an endless publish loop. It is now skipped as unsupported on both the snapshot and live paths. The snapshot worker gets a copy of local ID-to-kind taken at snapshot-begin.\n- **Banner handling, now matching iOS** (`SyncIssueLifecycle`): there is a new notice slot.\n  - Once-per-join notices (skipped-unsupported, quota, invalid, object too large, background paused, chat replay full) now survive reconnects. Before, the hello-ack of the very snapshot that skipped a record cleared its own \"update TacMap\" banner.\n  - Dismiss no longer clears pinned issues: \"changes paused\" lasts until leave.\n  - Retry (button or foreground) now clears the stop banner, as iOS does.\n- I added a `SnapshotRecordClassifier` object so the unit has the same name as on iOS. Both the snapshot and live paths classify through it.\n\n**Tests added** (sync tests went from 316 to 353):\n- **`SyncManagerSp2Test`** (18 tests through the real manager): quota and invalid nacks are not resent after reconnect; storage nack retries on the ack schedule; three `session-replaced` nacks stop sync; `hello-required` reconnects; live poison records are skipped; the path-change probe; 1013/1011 busy and 429 rate-limit floors and messages; 500 own unconfirmed tombstones stay under 150 frames per 10 s and the relay's 200 per fixed window, at most 32 in flight, with no duplicates; plus the defects above.\n- **`SyncHostileRecordFixtureTest`**: builds a hostile record for every skip reason in the fixture lists. Each is checked for its exact reason, then run through the real snapshot and live handlers. Two fatal rows that weren't scripted before are added.\n- **`SyncClientScenarioFixtureTest`**: drives the stale-nack, counter-window, poison-record, structural-stop, layer-ordering and Android-pause scenarios from the fixture's own steps. It also has a registry that fails if any of the 41 scenario IDs has no Android test.\n- **`SyncTransportCloseSignalTest`**: confirms on the real Java-WebSocket transport, against a local server, that upgrade statuses (503, 401, 429) and a 4013 close code reach the classifier.\n- `SyncOutboundPacerTest` and 2 new `SyncIssueLifecycleTest` cases.\n\n**Docs** (contract section 25): ADR-001 \u00a78, \u00a79, \u00a710, \u00a715 and \u00a716 now cover D3, D5, D6, D7 and D10. ADR-004 \u00a75 covers D8 and THREAT_MODEL \u00a77 covers D2. These are the SP2 relaxations the WIP code already shipped without their doc changes. Code comments follow the house style. No new