 "summary": "I finished iOS SP2 on top of WIP commit 79eb183 plus the mainline merge. The full iOS unit suite passes: 747 tests run, 0 failures, 1 skipped (the existing live-relay test that only runs when a relay is configured). The app and test targets build.\n\n**Getting it to compile.** Only one test broke against the merged mainline. `MGRSGridBehaviourTests` passed a `peers:` argument to `updateOverlays`, and SP3 contract 20.2 removed that parameter when presence moved into its own model. I deleted the argument and nothing else; it was not testing presence.\n\n**One real defect, now fixed in `ios/TacticalMaps/Sync/SyncManager.swift` (`endSession`).** iOS learns a socket opened from a separate callback. If the relay accepts the connection and immediately closes it (for example 4013 room full or 4011 identity rejected), the close can arrive first. The client then treated it as a failed connection attempt and kept retrying, instead of stopping (and, for 4011, raising a SECURITY issue). It now counts the socket as opened if it has a close code or the upgrade returned 101. Two new tests failed before the fix (13 reconnect sockets in 10 minutes instead of a stop; 4011 shown as a connection issue) and pass now.\n\n**Audit of the remaining SP2 rules.** Everything else in sections 1-16 was already implemented in the WIP. 31 new tests in three files confirm it through the real `SyncManager` and the fake transport:\n- **`SyncHostileRecordTests.swift` (5 tests):** every snapshot category listed in the fixture (skip-unverified, skip-unsupported, fatal-structural) goes through the real inbound handler, both inside a snapshot and as a live frame. Each check confirms:\n  - the classifier rejects the record for exactly the named reason;\n  - nothing is applied, nothing reconnects and nothing stops, and the notice appears once;\n  - nothing was committed to replay state (an older valid record for the same object is still accepted afterwards).\n- **`SyncManagerContractTableTests.swift` (12 tests):** every row of the close-code, connection-status and own-close tables in the fixture. Own closes are classified by our reason, not by the close code the socket echoes back. It also checks the half-full pacer after 4008, and runs the stale-nack, counter-window (presence keeps flowing) and seq-regression scripts with values read from the fixture.\n- **`SyncManagerProveItTests.swift` (14 tests):**\n  - network changes: a liveness ping on network loss or interface swap, and slow backoff classes are not shortened;\n  - 500 own tombstones resent after hello-ack stay within 150 frames per 10 s and 32 in flight, keeping their reserved stamps;\n  - a lost ack retransmits the same frame, and the first op-ack resets backoff;\n  - a stale nack on our own recovery resend counts as confirmed, with no reconnect;\n  - the 4014 epoch doubles three times, then stops;\n  - quota is reported once per session;\n  - the 30 s stable-session boundary;\n  - a full inbound queue stops reading until the drain catches up.\n\n**Test plumbing changes.**\n- `SyncTestDoubles.swift`: the harness takes an optional `LocationService`, so presence can be tested.\n- `SyncClientBehaviourTests.swift`:\n  - the reachability scenario is now read from the fixture instead of hardcoded;\n  - the \"every SP2 scenario has an iOS runner\" check now confirms the named test methods actually exist.\n\n**Other checks.** `check_localizations.py` passes, and `gen_sync_client_behaviour.py --check` reports the fixture is up to date. No new strings were needed. I did not touch `android/`, `sync/`, testdata or docs.",
