 "summary": "I finished SP3 on Android. The full unit suite passes (893 tests, 0 failures, 1 skipped as before) and `:app:assembleDebug` builds. The localisation check passes, and no new user strings were needed.\n\n**What was already in the working tree.** I read the WIP commit and the uncommitted SP2 changes first. Several SP3 rules were already done and still hold:\n- batched replay transactions with an undo log instead of deep copies, a running high-water mark, and presence counters persisted in strides\n- the wire-id index, built once per session with one reusable HMAC instance\n- snapshot checks on a validation worker, with one commit sealed on the persistence worker\n- stationary presence suppression (20.1) and PBKDF2 off the UI thread with a join token (20.3)\n- background entry without retries, the wake-safe probe with a wake lock, the paused notification, and the background-peer chat gate (21.1-21.3, 21.5, 21.6)\n\n**What I added:**\n1. **Live batches (S5-03):** each batch now uses one id-indexed view of the stores for prior hashes and the identity-collision check. Before, every live record scanned the whole store. Hashes are computed once per view, and snapshot apply reuses the commit's view when the stores haven't changed since.\n2. **Diff export cache:** GeoJSON is cached per object, keyed on the exact object and layer-list instances. A diff pass after one edit on a 2k-object map now exports 1 object instead of 2000. The cache is cleared everywhere `lastContent` is, because it holds plaintext.\n3. **Background reconnect (21.4), built but switched off:** a drop no longer stops the location service. The next fix, or the network coming back, opens a presence-only session on the next spare hello epoch that the last foreground hello already put on disk, so nothing is written in background. The snapshot is drained without opening any record (only fence order and the 4 MiB cap are checked), then hello, hello-ack, and only presence frames go out. Attempts are at least 60 s apart, and the device pauses after 3 failures, with no spares left, an oversized snapshot, or lost eligibility. The new switch is `SyncEnvironment.backgroundReconnectEnabled`.\n4. **Docs:** I added D4 (presence counter strides and the crash floor) to ADR-001 section 8, plus `presenceFenceExact` in its state schema. The stride code was already live without that doc change. I added D9 (background peers can't receive chat, the sender is blocked, and the remaining gap) to ADR-004 sections 1 and 9 and its residual-risks list. I did not land D1.\n\n**Before/after measurements** (from `SyncEfficiencyTest`, with the old pattern re-run on the real sealed writes and fresh-Mac HMACs in the same test; timings from the isolated run, full parallel suite in brackets):\n\n| Case | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2k objects x 2k tombstones reverse lookup | 8,000,000 HMACs, ~8.4 s (10.4 s) extrapolated from 100 measured tombstones; audit said 8.2 s | 2,000 HMACs, 163 ms (219 ms) for the whole snapshot including crypto |\n| 800-record snapshot apply | 802 replay writes + 800 waypoint writes, 6.1 s (11.5 s) | 3 replay writes + 1 waypoint write, 187 ms (247 ms) |\n| 100 presence frames over 2k stamps | 100 full-state seals, ~43.7 MB, 0.75 s (1.0 s) | 7 writes, ~3.1 MB, 77 ms (101 ms) |\n| Diff pass after one edit | 2000 exports | 1 export |\n| 64 queued live puts | 128 replay + 64 waypoint writes (by the old rule, not re-run) | 2 + 1 |\n| First publish of 300 objects | 300 reservation writes (by the old rule, not re-run) | 1 |\n\nOther SP3 numbers: 2000 reservations on a 20k-stamp state take 21-51 ms with the undo log, against 1.8 s for the old deep copy alone. A stationary unit sends 4 location frames in 60 s; a moving one sends 13.\n\n**Tests.** New: `SyncBackgroundReconnectTest` (9 tests) plus two tests in existing files. I added a reconnect switch to the test harness and registered the new tests for the two background fixture scenarios. `SyncEfficiencyTest` was rewritten to add the in-run \"before\" baselines; its existing limits are unchanged and no pre-existing test was weakened. I did not touch `ios/`, `sync/` or `testdata/`.",
